Mirror the delete guard in the update path: restrict updates to seed (isDefault/SEED_IDS) spells to cosmetic fields only. Minimal change in FileSystemSpellRepository.update() (lines 259-281):

```ts
async update(id: string, data: Partial<Spell>): Promise<Spell> {
  await this.ensureInitialized();

  const existing = await this.findById(id);
  if (!existing) throw new NotFoundError('Spell', id);

  // Seed-library spells (isDefault) may only have cosmetic fields overridden
  // (color/icon). Their behavior contract (action/failMode/trigger/loopType/
  // maxIterations/skillRef) is fixed — mirrors the delete guard above.
  const isSeed = SEED_IDS.has(id) || existing.isDefault;
  const sanitized: Partial<Spell> = isSeed
    ? { color: data.color, icon: data.icon, name: data.name, description: data.description }
    : data;

  const updated: Spell = {
    ...existing,
    ...sanitized,
    id: existing.id,
    createdAt: existing.createdAt,
    updatedAt: Date.now(),
    isDefault: existing.isDefault,
  };

  const filePath = path.join(this.spellsDir, `${id}.json`);
  await atomicWriteFile(filePath, JSON.stringify(updated));
  this.cache.set(id, updated);
  this.logger.debug(`Updated spell: ${id}`);
  return updated;
}
```

(Strip undefined keys from `sanitized` if you want to avoid clobbering name/description with undefined — or only spread the keys that are present.) Alternatively, reject behavior-field edits outright with a ValidationError when isSeed && any of action/failMode/trigger/loopType/maxIterations/skillRef is present, matching the delete guard's hard-fail style. Either keeps the intended 'recolor a seed without forking' feature while preserving the failMode:'closed' security contract.

